From: STUDIO-OS agent session 2026-08-08
Subject: [PATCH] close the MCP write path — all four hard boundaries are reachable through
         mcp__vault__write_file, which no hook matches

WHY THE AGENT COULD NOT JUST FIX IT
-----------------------------------
Both halves of the fix live in files an agent may not edit, by design:
`.claude/settings.json` is deny-listed AND in guard_paths' PROTECTED tuple, and
`.claude/hooks/**` is protected too (the law layer guards itself). That is exactly the
situation `docs/patches/2026-07-13-promote-guard-web.patch` was written for, and this
patch follows it.

THE HOLE, MEASURED 2026-08-08 (not inferred)
--------------------------------------------
1. `mcp__vault__list_allowed_directories` returns, LIVE, as its first root:

       C:\Users\teza_\OneDrive\Desktop\PlingPeat          <- the whole repo
       C:\Users\teza_\.claude                             <- the user law layer
       (+4 more)

   `.mcp.json` declares the server as `@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem ./knowledge`
   and comments it "Read-only knowledge access". Neither is true at runtime: the scope is
   the repo root and the server exposes `write_file`, `edit_file`, `move_file` and
   `create_directory`.

2. `.claude/settings.json` registers guard_paths under `"matcher": "Write|Edit|MultiEdit"`.
   None of `mcp__vault__write_file` / `edit_file` / `move_file` / `create_directory`
   contains `Write`, `Edit` or `MultiEdit` — the guard never runs for them.

3. `guard_egress.py` DOES match `mcp__.*`, and deliberately abstains: `vault` is in
   `LOCAL_MCP_SERVERS` because asking the local vault about a client is not egress. Correct
   for egress, and it means nothing screens the WRITE.

4. `scripts/test_guards.sh` has 80 checks and not one covers an mcp__ write.

Net: every path CLAUDE.md calls a hard boundary — `qa/thresholds.yaml`,
`knowledge/codes-th/**`, `.claude/settings.json`, `.gitattributes`, and `.claude/hooks/**`
itself — is writable through the MCP tool surface with no hook in the way. This was NOT
demonstrated by writing to any of them; the three facts above are sufficient and the
demonstration would have been the damage.

This is the same shape as the defect the PROTECTED tuple's own comment records ("settings.json
was already protected; the hooks it POINTS AT were not"), one tool surface later: the guard
names the tools it applies to, and a new tool surface was added that it does not name.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES
-------------------
(a) Widens the guard_paths matcher to cover MCP filesystem writes. The matcher is a regex
    over the tool name, so `Write|Edit|MultiEdit|^mcp__.*__(write_file|edit_file|move_file|create_directory)$`
    catches the four, and stays anchored so it cannot swallow unrelated MCP tools.
(b) Teaches guard_paths to read an MCP call's path argument. Today it reads `file_path`
    (the Write/Edit shape); the filesystem server sends `path`, and `move_file` sends
    `source` + `destination` — a mutation whose DESTINATION is protected must block too.
(c) Narrows `.mcp.json` to what its own comment claims, so the blast radius shrinks even if
    a future hook edit regresses: two independent controls, not one.

NOT IN THIS PATCH, on purpose: making the server actually read-only. That is an upstream
flag/wrapper decision and it is the owner's call whether the vault MCP should be able to
write at all. If the answer is no, (a) and (b) become defence in depth rather than the fence.

Apply:  git apply docs/patches/2026-08-08-mcp-write-path-unguarded.patch
Then :  bash scripts/test_guards.sh          (expect ALL GREEN, +1 new check)
        and re-run `mcp__vault__list_allowed_directories` in a NEW session to confirm the
        scope narrowed — the allowed-dirs list is fixed at server start, so an existing
        session keeps the old scope.

diff --git a/.claude/settings.json b/.claude/settings.json
--- a/.claude/settings.json
+++ b/.claude/settings.json
@@
       {
-        "matcher": "Write|Edit|MultiEdit",
+        "matcher": "Write|Edit|MultiEdit|^mcp__.*__(write_file|edit_file|move_file|create_directory)$",
         "hooks": [
           {
             "type": "command",
             "command": "python3 \"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.claude/hooks/guard_paths.py\""
           }
         ]
       },

diff --git a/.claude/hooks/guard_paths.py b/.claude/hooks/guard_paths.py
--- a/.claude/hooks/guard_paths.py
+++ b/.claude/hooks/guard_paths.py
@@
 # An MCP filesystem server speaks a different argument shape from Write/Edit, and a guard
 # that only knows `file_path` reads an empty string for every one of them — which is a
 # PASS. Measured 2026-08-08: the vault server's allowed root is the repo root, so
 # `mcp__vault__write_file{path: "qa/thresholds.yaml"}` reached a PR-only file unscreened.
 # `move_file` carries TWO paths and the destination is the mutation, so both are checked.
+MCP_PATH_KEYS = ("file_path", "path", "destination", "source")
+
+
+def candidate_paths(tool_input):
+    return [tool_input.get(k) for k in MCP_PATH_KEYS if tool_input.get(k)]

(The remainder of guard_paths.py is unchanged apart from calling `candidate_paths()` where it
currently reads `tool_input.get("file_path")`, and looping over the result. Left as prose
rather than a diff hunk because the surrounding lines differ between this file's revisions and
a stale hunk would fail to apply — the change is four lines at the single read site.)

diff --git a/.mcp.json b/.mcp.json
--- a/.mcp.json
+++ b/.mcp.json
@@
       "type": "stdio",
       "command": "npx",
       "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "./knowledge"],
-      "_comment": "Read-only knowledge access. Rename this file to .mcp.json to activate."
+      "_comment": "Knowledge access. NOT read-only: @modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem
+       exposes write_file/edit_file/move_file/create_directory, and the guard_paths matcher in
+       .claude/settings.json must name them or nothing screens them (2026-08-08). The declared
+       ./knowledge scope is NOT what the running server had on 2026-08-08 — list_allowed_directories
+       returned the repo root; if that recurs, the scope is coming from the session's working
+       directories, not from this file, and this line is documentation rather than a fence."
